From 3e51f7f77b02d2d84207bf93c8cdcafdbd2caa8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: csolomon <charliesolomon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 10:49:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] egress-allowlist: refuse HTTP requests to hosts not in
 GAH_ALLOWED_HOSTS

Defence in depth under 0010-restrict-model-sources and the policy pack's
providers.json. Those decide what the registry exposes; this decides what
the process may connect to, whatever code built the request -- including
upstream code added after this patch series was written (#8).

GAH_ALLOWED_HOSTS: comma-separated hostname globs (`*.amazonaws.com`,
`api.anthropic.com`). Unset or empty = deny everything, the same posture
as GAH_BUILTIN_MODELS. A bare `*` switches the control off, which is what
bin/gah defaults to on a workstation. Hostnames only, case-insensitive.

Three hooks, because the process uses three HTTP stacks:

- undici, i.e. every fetch(): the Anthropic, OpenAI and Google SDKs, OAuth
  token exchange and refresh, pi's own calls. A dispatcher interceptor
  applied in configureHttpDispatcher(), so it is re-applied whenever
  upstream rebuilds the dispatcher (idle-timeout setting changes) and it
  works under EnvHttpProxyAgent: the interceptor sees the target origin,
  not the proxy.
- node:http / node:https request() and get(): the AWS SDK's NodeHttpHandler,
  which Bedrock uses when an HTTP proxy is configured. The request options
  still name the target host under the proxy agent.
- node:http2 connect(): the AWS SDK's default NodeHttp2Handler, which
  Bedrock uses when no proxy is configured. Without this hook the shared
  host's own inference path would have been the one left uncovered.

The node hooks patch the module objects once (CommonJS consumers such as
the AWS SDK read them at call time; syncBuiltinESMExports() updates ESM
importers). A refusal throws GahEgressError, which reaches the user as the
provider error, and is appended to the audit log as egress_blocked once
per host per process so SDK retries cannot flood it. The allowlist is read
per call, not cached, so a stale value cannot fail open.

New file core/gah-egress.ts carries the logic; http-dispatcher.ts gains an
import and a two-line hunk at the setGlobalDispatcher call.

Answers to the open questions on #8: OAuth needs platform.claude.com
(token refresh) alongside api.anthropic.com, and the docs say so; package
install/update checks and the version check are already off (0013);
proxies work because both hooks inspect the target, not the connection;
the RPC entry point calls configureHttpDispatcher() too, so RPC and web-UI
sessions are covered.

Tests: test/gah-egress.test.ts (new) against a local server -- glob
semantics, deny-all/`*`, fetch blocked with the error as cause and audited
once, node:http and node:http2 blocked, allowlisted hosts pass on every
path, and the allowlist re-read per request. End to end with the built
binary: with GAH_ALLOWED_HOSTS empty a real session failed at the OAuth
refresh to platform.claude.com with the policy error and one audit line;
with api.anthropic.com,platform.claude.com the same request reached the
server (which answered 400: the local refresh token had expired).

